      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "false",
      "layering_notes": "San Joaquin County has no county housing trust fund, no county housing bond and no inclusionary ordinance; the County's own capital for multifamily is limited to HUD pass-through funds administered by the Human Services Agency Neighborhood Preservation Division (sjchsa.org), while the regional gap lender is the SJCOG-sponsored San Joaquin Regional Housing Fund and the main voucher and development partner is the Housing Authority of the County of San Joaquin (HACSJ). HHAP has been used as a small capital and operating piece in County-area PSH (for example $1,000,000 in Lodi Commons and $1,000,000 HHAP-5 for the BeWell tiny home village), but the HHAP-6 regional plan budgets $0 for capital and shifts money to sustaining operations, so developers should treat HHAP as an operating or services source for existing PSH rather than a construction source. The 2025 Regional Homeless Action Plan reports 240 PSH units built in the past five years and 200 plus in the pipeline, and a shelter inventory growing from 1,346 to 1,934 beds by the end of 2026. HHAP rounds 1 through 6 brought roughly $82.7 million to the region. HUD CoC Program funds flow through the SJCoC annual competition (2024 announcement posted on sjchsa.org); Stockton, the County and the CoC file a joint HHAP action plan.",
